<!--ID=FCB40C2B-1F9F-4C26-B1A1-CF8E67BE07D1;Version=1|{"Files":{}}-->
<!--ID=5B95B1CC-2C7B-494F-B746-CF22A0E779B7;Version=1|{"Locales":{"en-US":{"AutoApplyCustomLexiconFiles":[{}]},"en-GB":{"AutoApplyCustomLexiconFiles":[{}]}}}-->
<speak xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2001/10/synthesis"
       xmlns:mstts="http://www.w3.org/2001/mstts"
       xmlns:emo="http://www.w3.org/2009/10/emotionml"
       version="1.0"
       xml:lang="en-GB">
  <voice name="en-GB-OllieMultilingualNeural">
    <prosody rate="-10.25%" pitch="-2.25%">
            Comfort, comfort my people, says your God.
            <break time="1000ms"/>
            Speak tenderly to her, and cry to her that her warfare is ended, that her iniquity is pardoned.
            <break time="3800ms"/>
            Twice. He says it twice.
            <break time="1800ms"/>
            Comfort, comfort.
            <break time="1800ms"/>
            In the Hebrew tradition, repetition is not accident. It is intensity. It is emphasis reaching for something that one word alone could not carry. God says comfort twice because He means it more than once. Because He is not satisfied until it has reached the deep places — the places where pain has settled in and made itself at home.
            <break time="3800ms"/>
            My people.
            <break time="1800ms"/>
            Those two words carry everything. These are not strangers He is addressing. This is not a general announcement for anyone within earshot. This is spoken to the ones He has claimed. The ones He has walked with. The ones who belong to Him and who He belongs to.
            <break time="3800ms"/>
            Speak tenderly.
            <break time="1800ms"/>
            The Hebrew here literally means speak to the heart. God does not shout comfort from a distance. He leans in. He comes close. And He speaks to the place where the hurt actually lives — not around it, not over it, but directly into it.
            <break time="3800ms"/>
            Your warfare is ended.
            <break time="1800ms"/>
            The hard season has a limit. The struggle has a closing. What has felt like it would go on forever — it will not go on forever. God speaks an end to it. He declares its conclusion before it has finished, because He is the one who decides when it ends.
            <break time="3800ms"/>
            Her iniquity is pardoned.
            <break time="1800ms"/>
            Released. Cleared. Not held against her. The ledger is not being tallied. The debt is not accumulating. What was owed has been dealt with. What was heavy has been lifted.
            <break time="1800ms"/>
            Comfort, comfort.
            <break time="1000ms"/>
            Let that word find the places in you where it is most needed tonight.
    </prosody>
  </voice>
</speak>
